WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.814 --> 00:00:22.636
Wednesday of every month was always a "Perfectly Awful Day" at the John Grier Home for Orphans.

2
00:00:24.337 --> 00:00:27.138
That's when the trustees and the ladies' committee visited us.

3
00:00:29.799 --> 00:00:31.820
Of course, we gave them tea and sandwiches.

4
00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.741
But this was more than a social call.

5
00:00:37.562 --> 00:00:39.783
These men and women were also inspecting us.

6
00:00:45.887 --> 00:00:48.468
Everything at our orphanage had to be perfect.

7
00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:50.750
There was so much work to do!

8
00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:56.273
And as the oldest orphan, I—Jerusha Abbott—did most of it.

9
00:00:58.254 --> 00:01:01.856
One Wednesday I was on my feet at five o'clock in the morning.

10
00:01:05.468 --> 00:01:08.349
I mopped floors, dusted chairs, and made beds.

11
00:01:11.751 --> 00:01:15.252
I helped to scrub and dress 97 squirming orphans.

12
00:01:17.073 --> 00:01:18.714
I also reminded them to behave.

13
00:01:18.734 --> 00:01:22.536
"If a trustee or lady speaks to you, what do you say?"

14
00:01:23.456 --> 00:01:26.157
The children looked at each other and burst into giggles.

15
00:01:29.679 --> 00:01:31.900
"You say, 'Yes, sir' or 'No, ma'am.'

16
00:01:33.472 --> 00:01:36.394
Remember your manners and be polite to anyone you meet today."

17
00:01:38.815 --> 00:01:42.557
Mrs. Lippett, the matron at the orphanage, rushed me through these extra chores.

18
00:01:44.578 --> 00:01:45.979
She appeared calm to our guests.

19
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.361
But behind the scenes, she was often quite frantic.

20
00:01:52.842 --> 00:01:56.604
This Wednesday, like all the others, finally dragged to a close.

21
00:01:59.066 --> 00:02:02.928
As our guests were leaving, I returned to my regular duties in Room F.

22
00:02:05.493 --> 00:02:08.755
I was in charge of eleven little girls, ages four to seven.

23
00:02:10.095 --> 00:02:11.036
It was time for supper.

24
00:02:12.257 --> 00:02:14.578
So I straightened their dresses and wiped their noses.

25
00:02:15.939 --> 00:02:17.520
Then I sent them off to the dining room.

26
00:02:22.283 --> 00:02:24.524
I stayed upstairs and sank into a window seat.

27
00:02:26.165 --> 00:02:28.786
I pressed my aching head against the cool glass.

28
00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:31.128
The day seemed like a success.

29
00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:36.623
The ladies and gentlemen had made their rounds, read their reports, and sipped their tea.

30
00:02:38.684 --> 00:02:43.287
From my seat, I watched their carriages and automobiles rolling out of the orphanage gates.

31
00:02:45.268 --> 00:02:48.930
In my mind I followed them to the big houses dotting the hills.

32
00:02:51.792 --> 00:02:54.814
I imagined myself riding home in one of those carriages.

33
00:02:56.255 --> 00:02:58.076
I wore a fur coat and a velvet hat.

34
00:03:00.344 --> 00:03:04.387
But then I tried to picture the inside of my imaginary house and I couldn't.

35
00:03:06.288 --> 00:03:10.871
That was because I'd never been in a real house in all my 18 years.

36
00:03:10.891 --> 00:03:17.176
"Je-ru-sha Ab-bott, you are want-ed in the of-fice.

37
00:03:17.736 --> 00:03:19.798
And I think you'd better hurry up!"

38
00:03:19.838 --> 00:03:21.679
Tommy Dillon came singing up the stairs.

39
00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:26.162
He'd recently joined the choir, so he sang all the time.

40
00:03:27.403 --> 00:03:28.904
I pulled myself away from the window.

41
00:03:29.728 --> 00:03:30.429
"Who wants me?"

42
00:03:30.769 --> 00:03:31.850
I asked anxiously.

43
00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:36.914
"Mrs. Lip-pett and I think she's mad."

44
00:03:39.236 --> 00:03:40.998
"Oh no!"

45
00:03:41.418 --> 00:03:42.019
I groaned.

46
00:03:42.039 --> 00:03:44.261
"What did I do?

47
00:03:44.281 --> 00:03:47.183
Were the sandwiches too thick today?

48
00:03:48.084 --> 00:03:50.947
Did a lady visitor see the hole in an orphan's stocking?"

49
00:03:52.288 --> 00:03:52.988
Tommy shrugged.

50
00:03:54.289 --> 00:03:56.692
He had no idea, but he followed me downstairs.

51
00:03:58.668 --> 00:04:00.609
The long lower hallway was dark.

52
00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:04.771
One last trustee stood in the open door.

53
00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:07.633
He was a tall man.

54
00:04:08.694 --> 00:04:11.175
He motioned for an automobile to pull up to the door.

55
00:04:12.836 --> 00:04:17.418
As the automobile approached, its headlights cast the man's shadow against the wall.

56
00:04:18.499 --> 00:04:20.860
The shadow had very long arms and legs.

57
00:04:22.521 --> 00:04:25.403
Suddenly my anxiety melted, and I began to laugh.

58
00:04:28.972 --> 00:04:32.494
"That shadow looks just like a giant daddy longlegs!"

59
00:04:32.514 --> 00:04:33.434
I whispered to Tommy.

60
00:04:35.755 --> 00:04:36.415
He laughed too.

61
00:04:36.435 --> 00:04:40.797
"Good luck!"

62
00:04:41.397 --> 00:04:43.238
he sang as he left me outside Mrs.

63
00:04:43.298 --> 00:04:43.898
Lippett's door.

64
00:04:45.139 --> 00:04:46.619
I can find the humor in most things.

65
00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:51.321
So, still smiling about Daddy Longlegs, I knocked on Mrs.

66
00:04:51.381 --> 00:04:51.982
Lippett's door.

67
00:04:53.942 --> 00:04:57.444
Much to my surprise, the matron looked almost as cheerful as I did.

68
00:04:59.848 --> 00:05:01.089
"Sit down, Jerusha.

69
00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:02.831
I have something to say to you."

70
00:05:05.033 --> 00:05:06.574
I dropped into the nearest chair.

71
00:05:07.415 --> 00:05:09.296
An automobile flashed past the window.

72
00:05:10.157 --> 00:05:11.378
Mrs. Lippett glanced at it.

73
00:05:11.398 --> 00:05:14.701
"Did you notice the gentleman who just left?"

74
00:05:14.721 --> 00:05:16.982
"I only saw his back."

75
00:05:18.344 --> 00:05:20.185
"He's one of our wealthiest trustees.

76
00:05:21.566 --> 00:05:24.629
Over the years he has given large sums of money to the orphanage.

77
00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:27.812
But I'm not allowed to mention his name."

78
00:05:29.568 --> 00:05:30.528
I didn't know what to say.

79
00:05:31.589 --> 00:05:35.050
I wasn't used to talking to her about trustees and their strange requests.

80
00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:39.991
"In the past he has taken an interest in several of our boys.

81
00:05:41.012 --> 00:05:43.692
Do you remember Charles Benton or Henry Freize?

82
00:05:45.613 --> 00:05:50.174
Mr.—I mean, this trustee—sent them both through college.

83
00:05:51.195 --> 00:05:53.495
They repaid him with hard work and success.

84
00:05:54.176 --> 00:05:55.516
That's all he required."

85
00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.704
The matron glanced at the window again, as if expecting the gentleman to return.

86
00:06:01.724 --> 00:06:05.266
"So far he has only helped boys.

87
00:06:06.367 --> 00:06:08.849
I've never been able to interest him in helping girls.

88
00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:12.092
No matter how smart they were.

89
00:06:13.053 --> 00:06:14.274
I guess he dislikes girls."

90
00:06:15.134 --> 00:06:16.115
Mrs. Lippett paused.

91
00:06:17.656 --> 00:06:19.338
She seemed to expect a reply here.

92
00:06:20.378 --> 00:06:22.420
So I said the first thing that came into my head.

93
00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:23.781
"Yes, ma'am."

94
00:06:25.422 --> 00:06:26.303
Satisfied, Mrs.

95
00:06:26.363 --> 00:06:27.144
Lippett continued.

96
00:06:28.544 --> 00:06:31.666
"Today at our meeting, the question of your future came up."

97
00:06:31.686 --> 00:06:35.108
"Yes, ma'am," I replied politely.

98
00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:41.111
Inside I was filled with a mix of curiosity—and dread.
